+### PRD: PRD - PM creates combined PRD and feature list (draft)
+
+Product Requirements Document (PRD)  
+Project Name: Account Creation  
+Document Date: May 27, 2026  
+Author: Product Team  
+
+1. Executive Summary  
+
+This document outlines the requirements for developing a Salesforce Screen Flow that streamlines and guides users through the process of creating new Account records. The goal is to improve data accuracy, enforce required fields, reduce user errors, and provide a guided, user-friendly experience for Account creation within Salesforce.
+
+The proposed solution leverages Salesforce Screen Flow to present a structured, validated, and secure interface for users to enter Account information. Upon successful submission, a new Account record will be created in Salesforce, and the user will receive confirmation. In case of errors, users will be presented with clear and actionable error messages.
+
+2. Problem Statement  
+
+Currently, users may create Account records directly through standard Salesforce page layouts. This can lead to:
+  - Inconsistent data entry.
+  - Missing critical required fields.
+  - Increased user confusion when encountering validation errors.
+  - Limited guidance during record creation.
+
+A guided flow experience will ensure that required information is consistently captured, input validation is enforced before record creation, and users receive clear feedback throughout the process.
+
+3. Goals and Objectives  
+
+3.1 Primary Goals  
+  - Provide a structured, guided experience for creating new Account records.  
+  - Ensure all required fields (e.g., Account Name, Type, Industry) are captured.  
+  - Reduce data entry errors through proactive validation.  
+  - Improve user confidence and efficiency during Account creation.  
+
+3.2 Success Metrics  
+  - 100 percent of Accounts created via the flow include required fields.  
+  - Reduction in Account-related validation errors by at least 50 percent within 60 days of release.  
+  - Positive user feedback (average satisfaction score ≥ 4 out of 5 in post-launch survey).  
+  - Flow completion rate ≥ 95 percent.  
+
+4. Scope  
+
+4.1 In Scope  
+  - Development of a Salesforce Screen Flow for Account creation.  
+  - Input fields for required Account fields (e.g., Account Name, Type, Industry).  
+  - Validation to ensure required fields are not left blank.  
+  - Creation of a new Account record using Flow Create Records element.  
+  - Success confirmation message screen.  
+  - Error handling for record creation failure.  
+  - Deployment to production environment.  
+  - Assignment of proper permissions to relevant user profiles or permission sets.  
+
+4.2 Out of Scope  
+  - Custom Apex development (unless required for exception handling).  
+  - Integration with external systems.  
+  - Bulk Account creation via CSV or API.  
+  - Changes to Account data model beyond required configuration updates.  
+  - Advanced automation triggered post-creation (e.g., opportunity auto-creation).  
+
+5. Stakeholders  
+  - Product Manager: Owns requirements and prioritization.  
+  - CTO: Technical oversight and architecture approval.  
+  - Salesforce Administrator: Configuration and deployment.  
+  - Sales Operations: Primary business stakeholder.  
+  - End Users: Sales and service users creating Accounts.  
+  - QA Team: Testing and validation.  
+
+6. User Personas  
+
+6.1 Sales Representative  
+  - Creates new Accounts frequently.  
+  - Requires quick, guided entry.  
+  - Needs assurance that records are saved correctly.  
+
+6.2 Sales Operations Manager  
+  - Ensures data quality and completeness.  
+  - Requires consistent classification of Accounts (Type, Industry).  
+
+7. Functional Requirements  
+
+7.1 Flow Entry  
+
+FR-1: The system shall provide a Screen Flow accessible from:  
+  - A custom button or action on the Account object.  
+  - The Salesforce App Launcher or Utility Bar (to be finalized during configuration).  
+
+FR-2: Only users with appropriate object permissions (Create on Account) shall be able to launch and complete the flow.  
+
+7.2 Data Collection Screen  
+
+FR-3: The flow shall prompt the user for the following required fields:  
+  - Account Name (Text)  
+  - Type (Picklist)  
+  - Industry (Picklist)  
+
+FR-4: The flow shall dynamically use the Account object’s field definitions to ensure alignment with metadata (e.g., picklist values).  
+
+FR-5: Additional optional fields may be included (e.g., Phone, Website, Billing Address), subject to stakeholder confirmation.  
+
+7.3 Input Validation  
+
+FR-6: The flow shall enforce that Account Name is not left blank.  
+
+FR-7: Required fields (Account Name, Type, Industry) shall be configured as mandatory on the screen component level.  
+
+FR-8: If a required field is not completed, the user shall not be allowed to proceed and shall see inline validation messages.  
+
+FR-9: Field-level validation rules defined on the Account object shall also be respected.  
+
+7.4 Record Creation  
+
+FR-10: Upon successful validation, the flow shall create a new Account record using the Create Records element.  
+
+FR-11: The flow shall map each screen input field to its corresponding Account field.  
+
+FR-12: The Account record shall be created under the context of the running user and respect sharing rules and field-level security.  
+
+7.5 Confirmation and Error Handling  
+
+FR-13: Upon successful creation, the user shall see a confirmation screen with:  
+  - A success message stating that the Account was created successfully.  
+  - The Account Name displayed.  
+  - A link to the newly created Account record.  
+
+FR-14: If the record creation fails due to validation rules, missing permissions, or system errors, the flow shall:  
+  - Display a clear error message.  
+  - Avoid exposing technical stack traces.  
+  - Provide guidance to contact an administrator if the issue persists.  
+
+FR-15: The flow shall include a fault path from the Create Records element to an error screen.  
+
+8. Non-Functional Requirements  
+
+8.1 Performance  
+  - The flow should load within 2 seconds under normal system conditions.  
+  - Record creation should complete within standard Salesforce response times.  
+
+8.2 Security  
+  - The solution must respect Salesforce security model including:  
+  - Object-level permissions.  
+  - Field-Level Security (FLS).  
+  - Organization-Wide Defaults (OWD).  
+  - No elevation of privilege shall occur through the flow.  
+
+8.3 Usability  
+  - The flow shall use clear labels and field-level help text.  
+  - The layout shall be clean and logically grouped.  
+  - Error messages shall be written in plain business language.  
+
+8.4 Maintainability  
+  - The flow shall be built using best practices in Flow naming conventions and documentation.  
+  - Flow API name example: Account_Creation_Screen_Flow.  
+  - All elements shall have meaningful labels and descriptions.  
+
+9. Data Model Considerations  
+  - The solution will leverage the standard Account object.  
+  - Required fields shall align with the Account object configuration.  
+  - Any new required field introduced at the object level must be reflected in the flow.  
+  - Field-Level Security must be reviewed to ensure users can access required fields.  
+
+10. Technical Design Overview  
+
+10.1 Architecture  
+  - Salesforce Screen Flow (Auto-Layout recommended for maintainability).  
+  - Create Records element for Account object.  
+  - Fault connectors for error handling.  
+
+10.2 Deployment  
+  - Developed in a sandbox environment.  
+  - Tested in a full or partial sandbox.  
+  - Deployed via Change Sets or Salesforce DX (to be defined).  
+
+10.3 Governor Limits  
+  - Minimal DML operations (single record creation).  
+  - No loops or unnecessary queries.  
+  - Designed to operate within standard Flow limits.  
+
+11. Assumptions  
+  - Users have Create permission on Account.  
+  - Required fields are limited to Account Name, Type, and Industry unless otherwise specified.  
+  - No external integrations are required at launch.  
+  - Standard Salesforce Account object is in use (no custom object replacement).  
+
+12. Risks and Mitigation  
+
+Risk: Additional required fields may be added later.  
+Mitigation: Build flow to easily accommodate additional required inputs.  
+
+Risk: Conflicts with existing validation rules.  
+Mitigation: Conduct validation rule review prior to deployment.  
+
+Risk: User resistance to change.  
+Mitigation: Provide training and in-app guidance.  
+
+13. Release Plan  
+
+Phase 1: Requirements Finalization  
+  - Stakeholder validation of required and optional fields.  
+
+Phase 2: Build  
+  - Flow configuration in sandbox.  
+  - Security and permission review.  
+
+Phase 3: Testing  
+  - Unit testing by admin.  
+  - UAT with Sales users.  
+  - Validation of error handling scenarios.  
+
+Phase 4: Deployment  
+  - Production deployment via approved deployment method.  
+  - Post-deployment smoke testing.  
+
+Phase 5: Post-Release Monitoring  
+  - Monitor flow error rates.  
+  - Collect user feedback.  
+
+14. Acceptance Criteria  
+
+AC-1: The flow prompts for Account Name, Type, and Industry.  
+AC-2: The flow prevents submission when Account Name is blank.  
+AC-3: Upon successful completion, a new Account record is created in Salesforce.  
+AC-4: The user sees a confirmation message upon successful creation.  
+AC-5: If record creation fails, the user sees a clear and user-friendly error message.  
+
+15. Open Questions  
+  - Should additional fields be mandatory at launch?  
+  - Should the flow replace the standard “New Account” button entirely?  
+  - Should post-creation automation (e.g., task creation) be included in a future phase?  
+
+16. Conclusion  
+
+The Account Creation Screen Flow will provide a structured, validated, and user-friendly mechanism for creating new Account records in Salesforce. By guiding users through required fields, enforcing validation rules, and providing clear success and error messaging, the solution will improve data quality, reduce user errors, and enhance overall system usability.  
+
+This PRD serves as the foundation for design, build, testing, and deployment activities for the Account Creation project.
+
+---
+
