+## Output Requirements
+
+Generate complete, production-ready Salesforce code (Apex classes, triggers,
+LWC components, metadata) for the SFDX project structure under force-app/.
+Write every file described in the LLD. No stubs or placeholders.
+
+APEX COMPILE RULES — automated gate, violations trigger IMMEDIATE REJECTION and re-run:
+1. NEVER use __c on Apex variable names, parameter names, or class member names.
+   __c is ONLY valid in SOQL field references (inside [ ]) and .field-meta.xml <fullName> tags.
+   WRONG: private String accountId__c;   RIGHT: private String accountId;
+2. Every Apex class and trigger must have perfectly balanced braces { }.
+3. @isTest classes must NOT use extends or implements.
+4. All public/global Apex classes must declare: with sharing | without sharing | inherited sharing.
+5. Every .flow-meta.xml with a <start> element must have:
+   <connector><targetReference>FirstElementName</targetReference></connector>
+
+SFDX STRUCTURAL RULES — automated preflight runs after generation; violations REJECT and re-run:
+6. Every Apex class file MUST have a paired -meta.xml file with apiVersion + status:
+   force-app/main/default/classes/Foo.cls          ← code
+   force-app/main/default/classes/Foo.cls-meta.xml ← REQUIRED companion
+   The -meta.xml must contain <ApexClass><apiVersion>62.0</apiVersion><status>Active</status></ApexClass>.
+   Same rule for triggers: every Foo.trigger needs Foo.trigger-meta.xml with <ApexTrigger> root.
+7. Every Lightning Web Component bundle MUST contain ALL THREE files with the SAME basename:
+   force-app/main/default/lwc/myComponent/myComponent.js
+   force-app/main/default/lwc/myComponent/myComponent.html
+   force-app/main/default/lwc/myComponent/myComponent.js-meta.xml
+   Missing any of the three causes "INCOMPLETE_LWC_BUNDLE" rejection. Apex controllers are NOT part of the bundle.
+8. Every XML file MUST be well-formed: every opening tag has a matching closing tag, no truncated output.
+   Pay special attention to nested permission-set tags: <PermissionSet>, <fieldPermissions>, <field>, <objectPermissions>.
+   If you emit <fieldPermissions>, you MUST close it with </fieldPermissions> before closing <PermissionSet>.
+   Required-field, picklist value, and object metadata XML must close every tag explicitly.
+9. Field-level metadata: a single field is declared in EXACTLY ONE place per object — either
+   inline inside Object__c.object-meta.xml under <fields>...</fields>, OR in a separate file at
+   objects/Object__c/fields/Field__c.field-meta.xml. NEVER both — that produces "duplicate field"
+   deploy errors. The separate-file form is preferred for complex fields (picklists, formulas).
+10. Custom object Object__c.object-meta.xml MUST declare <sharingModel> exactly once.
+    Valid values for standalone objects: Private | Read | ReadWrite.
+    CRITICAL: For MasterDetail child objects, do NOT include <sharingModel> at all.
+    Salesforce automatically derives ControlledByParent from the MasterDetail relationship.
+    Explicitly writing <sharingModel>ControlledByParent</sharingModel> causes a deploy error:
+    "Cannot set sharingModel for this object type." Omit it entirely on child objects.
+11. If a feature reuses a custom object/field defined in another feature, REFERENCE it; do not
+    re-declare. Re-declaring with a different <type> or <required> value triggers
+    "FIELD_TYPE_MISMATCH" / "FIELD_REQUIRED_MISMATCH" rejection at the cross-deliverable schema gate.
+12. Test class method signatures MUST EXACTLY MATCH production class methods.
+    Before writing any @isTest method, read the actual production class and list its method signatures.
+    WRONG: calling MetadataService.getProfileMetadata(String) when production has getProfileMetadata(String, Id).
+    WRONG: calling MyClass.logCallout(String, Integer, String) when that overload does not exist.
+    Every method call in a test MUST match the exact name, parameter count, and parameter types.
+    If the production class is an instance class, do not call its methods as static in the test.
+13. NEVER use Apex Metadata API types: Metadata.PermissionSet, Metadata.DeployContainer,
+    Metadata.Operations.enqueueDeployment, or any Metadata.* type.
+    These require "Enable Permissions management from Apex Metadata API" which is disabled in
+    most scratch orgs and sandboxes, causing compile-time "Invalid type: Metadata.PermissionSet".
+    Use PermissionSet SObject with DML (insert/update) instead.
+14. NAMESPACE PREFIX RULES — check sfdx-project.json for a "namespace" field (e.g. "ns"):
+    The namespace prefix rule is ABSOLUTE — it applies to ALL Apex contexts. Never write ns__ on
+    your own package types anywhere in Apex code. This covers EVERY context:
+    • Type declarations: Custom_Object__c obj — NOT ns__Custom_Object__c obj
+    • Field access: obj.Status__c — NOT obj.ns__Status__c
+    • Custom Metadata type names: Custom_Config__mdt cfg — NOT ns__Custom_Config__mdt cfg
+    • SOQL FROM clause: [SELECT Id FROM Custom_Object__c] — NOT FROM ns__Custom_Object__c
+    • SOQL SELECT fields: [SELECT Status__c FROM Custom_Object__c] — NOT SELECT ns__Status__c
+    Salesforce resolves ALL of these automatically at deploy time.
+    The ns__ prefix is ONLY valid in XML <fullName> tags inside metadata files.
+    • Inside XML metadata files (<fullName> tags): INCLUDE the full namespace prefix.
+      Write <fullName>ns__Custom_Object__c</fullName>, <fullName>ns__Status__c</fullName>.
+    WRONG: Apex code using ns__Status__c (prefix outside XML)
+    WRONG: SOQL FROM ns__Custom_Object__c (prefix in FROM clause)
+    WRONG: SOQL SELECT ns__Status__c (prefix in SELECT field list)
+    WRONG: ns__Custom_Config__mdt cfg as an Apex type declaration
+    WRONG: XML <fullName>Custom_Object__c</fullName> without prefix in a namespaced package
+15. ALL Apex field references must exist on the deployed object schema.
+    Before referencing obj.Status__c in Apex, verify Status__c is actually declared on the object.
+    If the object declares Action_Type__c, you MUST use obj.Action_Type__c in Apex.
+    Field name mismatches cause compile-time "Variable does not exist" errors.
+    Cross-check every Apex SObject field access against the object-meta.xml or field-meta.xml.
+    This includes SOQL SELECT lists — every field in SELECT must exist on the object.
+16. SFDX FILE PATH NAMING — NEVER include namespace prefix in SFDX source file paths.
+    File system paths must use BARE component names. The namespace is applied automatically.
+    WRONG: force-app/main/default/classes/ns__MyService.cls
+    RIGHT: force-app/main/default/classes/MyService.cls
+    WRONG: force-app/main/default/lwc/ns__myComponent/ns__myComponent.js
+    RIGHT: force-app/main/default/lwc/myComponent/myComponent.js
+    WRONG: force-app/main/default/permissionsets/ns__MyPermSet.permissionset-meta.xml
+    RIGHT: force-app/main/default/permissionsets/MyPermSet.permissionset-meta.xml
+    EXCEPTION: Object/field directories DO keep the prefix: objects/ns__MyObj__c/ is correct.
+    This applies to: classes/, triggers/, lwc/, aura/, permissionsets/, permissionsetgroups/.
+    Violation causes "Cannot create component with namespace" or "Invalid character in identifier".
+17. SHARED WORKSPACE — SCAN BEFORE CREATING ANY APEX CLASS:
+    All deliverables in this project share the SAME force-app/ directory. Another deliverable may
+    already have created the class you are about to write. Before creating ANY .cls file:
+      find force-app/main/default/classes -name "ClassName.cls"
+    File EXISTS → read existing content first, then ADD your methods — preserve all existing methods.
+    File NOT EXIST → create it fresh.
+    NEVER overwrite an existing class — you delete methods other classes depend on.
+    "Method does not exist" compile errors are almost always caused by class overwrites across deliverables.
+    Apply this scan to triggers and LWC components too before creating them.
+18. COMPLETE EACH XML FILE BEFORE STARTING THE NEXT:
+    Every XML file MUST end with its root closing tag before you begin the next === FILE: === block.
+      .field-meta.xml         → must end with </CustomField>
+      .object-meta.xml        → must end with </CustomObject>
+      .cls-meta.xml           → must end with </ApexClass>
+      .trigger-meta.xml       → must end with </ApexTrigger>
+      .permissionset-meta.xml → must end with </PermissionSet>
+    If running low on context: STOP after closing the current file. Never start a file you cannot finish.
+    One complete file is always better than two truncated files.
+    Truncated XML causes MALFORMED_XML at deploy time — no preflight tool can auto-repair it.
+
+## Pre-Codegen Advisory (heads-up only — NOT a hard gate)
+
+The LLD has the following gaps detected by static analysis. Each is INFORMATIONAL —
+the agent should proceed with reasonable defaults and the Sr Dev review will catch
+anything that drifts from intent. The advisory exists so the agent makes EXPLICIT,
+reviewable choices for each gap instead of silently inventing answers.
+
+ℹ **[LLD_NO_NAMED_COMPONENTS]** No named components detected (classes, services, handlers, triggers). The agent will invent component names — they may not match the team's preferred naming convention.
+
+
+
+════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
+## STAGE 1 — MANDATORY SELF-VALIDATION TOKEN BEFORE YOU EXIT
+════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
+
+🛑 HARD REQUIREMENT — this section overrides every other instruction:
+
+Your final output MUST contain a line that starts (after any leading
+whitespace or bullet marker) with the exact prefix `SELF_VALIDATION:`
+followed by one of `PASS`, `SKIP`, or `FAIL`. The orchestrator scans
+your stdout for this token; if it is ABSENT, the orchestrator parses
+`selfValidation = undefined` and downstream gates have no signal —
+this has caused 11+ wasted revision cycles on real projects.
+
+You MUST emit the token EVEN IF sfdx is not installed. The correct
+value when sfdx is unavailable is `SELF_VALIDATION: SKIP (sfdx
+unavailable)`. Silence is NOT an option.
+
+### ✅ Salesforce org IS pre-authenticated for this session
+   Alias: **klagent-dev-4689875b** — already set up via `sf org login access-token` by the orchestrator.
+   Use `--target-org klagent-dev-4689875b` on every sfdx invocation; do NOT try to authenticate yourself.
+
+### Decision tree (pick exactly one and emit it):
+
+  • sfdx CLI not in PATH → `SELF_VALIDATION: SKIP (sfdx unavailable)`
+  • No Salesforce org credentials in env → `SELF_VALIDATION: SKIP (no org auth)`
+  • Check-only deploy passed → `SELF_VALIDATION: PASS (attempts=<N>)`
+  • Check-only deploy failed all 5 attempts → `SELF_VALIDATION: FAIL (attempts=5, unresolved=<count>)`
+
+### When sfdx IS available, run the fix-revalidate loop:
+1. `sfdx force:source:deploy --checkonly --testlevel NoTestRun --manifest manifest/package.xml --json --target-org klagent-dev-4689875b`
+2. Parse JSON output. If `result.success === false`:
+   a. Read `result.details.componentFailures` (fileName, problemType, problem, lineNumber).
+   b. Fix every component error by editing the affected files.
+   c. Re-run step 1, increment attempt counter.
+3. Cap at 5 attempts. After 5 failures, emit FAIL with the unresolved error list verbatim.
+
+### Hard rules during Stage 1:
+- Do NOT add new components — only fix errors the check-only deploy reports.
+- Do NOT run a real deploy (omit `--checkonly` would deploy for real — forbidden).
+- Do NOT exit your session without emitting one of the four token forms above.
+
+⚠️  REMINDER: this is the LAST instruction in your prompt by design.
+   The orchestrator placed it last because CLI agents weight the final
+   instruction heaviest. Treat the SELF_VALIDATION line as a
+   non-negotiable output requirement, on par with the === FILE: ===
+   delimiter that defines your file output format.
+
+
+
+════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
+## STAGE 1.5 — MANDATORY APEX SYNTAX SELF-VALIDATION TOKEN BEFORE YOU EXIT
+════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
+
+🛑 HARD REQUIREMENT — this section runs alongside Stage 1 (sfdx):
+
+Your final output MUST contain a line that starts (after any leading
+whitespace or bullet marker) with the exact prefix `APEX_SYNTAX:`
+followed by one of `PASS`, `SKIP`, or `FAIL`. This is a SECOND
+mandatory token — distinct from `SELF_VALIDATION:` — that gates a
+deterministic static lint pass (no Salesforce org required, faster
+than the sfdx check-only). It catches the defect class that has
+been escalating projects to pending_human_review for the last week
+(fabricated SObject/field refs, malformed XML, missing meta-xml,
+incomplete LWC bundles).
+
+### Decision tree (pick exactly one and emit it):
+
+  • Script unavailable (e.g. tsx not installed) → `APEX_SYNTAX: SKIP (script unavailable)`
+  • Static lint passed → `APEX_SYNTAX: PASS (attempts=<N>)`
+  • Static lint failed after 5 fix attempts → `APEX_SYNTAX: FAIL (attempts=5, errors=<count>)`
+
+### Fix-revalidate loop:
+1. Run: `npx tsx scripts/apex-syntax-check.ts`
+2. Parse the JSON output on stdout. Schema:
+   `{ "ok": boolean, "errors": number, "warnings": number,`
+   ` "violations": [{ "rule", "severity", "filePath", "line", "message" }, ...] }`
+3. If `ok` is `true` → emit `APEX_SYNTAX: PASS (attempts=<N>)` and stop.
+4. If `ok` is `false`:
+   a. Read each error-severity violation's `filePath` + `line` + `message`.
+   b. Fix the affected files surgically (do NOT rewrite unrelated code).
+   c. Re-run step 1, increment attempt counter (max 5).
+5. After 5 failures, emit `APEX_SYNTAX: FAIL` with the unresolved count.
+
+### Common violation rules and remediation:
+- `UNDECLARED_SHARING_MODEL` → add `with sharing` / `without sharing` / `inherited sharing` to class declaration
+- `INVALID_NAMESPACE_PREFIX_IN_APEX` → strip `<ns>__` prefix from Apex identifiers (it is XML-only)
+- `MISSING_COMPANION_METAXML` → emit the matching `.cls-meta.xml` / `.trigger-meta.xml` for the affected source file
+- `INCOMPLETE_LWC_BUNDLE` → emit all three of `.html`, `.js`, `.js-meta.xml` for the LWC bundle
+- `MALFORMED_XML` → re-emit the file end-to-end; never truncate XML mid-tag
+
+### Hard rules during Stage 1.5:
+- Run this BEFORE Stage 1's `SELF_VALIDATION:` token — apex-syntax gate is faster
+  and catches more cheaply.
+- Do NOT skip this even if Stage 1 already passed; the two gates are orthogonal.
+- Emit one of the three token forms above EVEN IF the script is unavailable.
+

# 2026-05-27 02:29:15.701758
+# ASDA Developer Agent — Salesforce Code Implementation Task
+
+Project: Account Creation
+Description: Develop a Salesforce Screen Flow to streamline and guide users through the process of creating new Account records.
+
+Acceptance Criteria:
+1. The screen flow must prompt the user for all required fields for a new Account, such as Account Name, Type, and Industry.
+2. Input validation must be in place to ensure critical data, like the Account Name, is not left blank.
+3. Upon successful completion of the flow, a new Account record is created in Salesforce with the entered data.
+4. The user is presented with a confirmation message upon successful creation.
+5. If the record creation fails for any reason, a clear error message is displayed to the user.
+Client: Keneland  (Technology)
+
+## Implementation Goal
+
+---
+Low-Level Design Document
+
+Project: Account Creation
+Document Date: May 27, 2026
+Author: Development Team
+
+1. Introduction
+
+1.1 Purpose
+The purpose of this Low-Level Design (LLD) document is to provide detailed specifications and design considerations for implementing the "Account Creation" Salesforce Screen Flow project. The document outlines the architecture, components, interface designs, and procedural logic necessary to meet the requirements specified in the Product Requirements Document (PRD).
+
+1.2 Scope
+This document covers the design and technical specifications necessary for developing a Salesforce Screen Flow that enables the creation of new Account records. The scope is confined to the creation, validation, and error handling related to the flow and the deployment strategy.
+
+2. System Architecture
+
+2.1 Overview
+The solution is built on the Salesforce Lightning Platform utilizing the Screen Flow interface. The architecture involves the use of Screen Flow components, configured validation, and interaction with standard Salesforce data models to create and manage Account records.
+
+2.2 Components
+  - Screen Flow: Serves as the primary interface for data entry and validation.
+  - Create Records Element: Utilized for persisting the Account data.
+  - Fault Path Logic: Handles exceptions and prompts user-friendly error messages.
+
+3. Flow Design
+
+3.1 Flow Entry
+The flow is accessible through multiple entry points:
+  - Custom Button: A button on the Account object provides direct access to the flow.
+  - Utility Bar/App Launcher: Users can initiate the flow from common Salesforce interface components.
+
+3.2 Data Collection Screens
+The flow involves one or more screens to collect requisite data:
+  - Screen 1: Captures the Account Name, Type, and Industry.
+  - Optional Screen(s): May include additional fields such as Phone and Website based on user roles or organizational requirements.
+
+3.3 Input Validation
+Validation is applied at multiple levels to ensure data accuracy:
+  - Inline Validation: Ensures the Account Name field is not empty and highlights incomplete fields with user-friendly messages.
+  - Metadata Synchronization: Captures values in alignment with object field definitions, particularly for picklists.
+
+4. Record Creation
+
+4.1 Element Configuration
+The Create Records element is configured to:
+  - Map Screen Inputs: Align each input with corresponding Account object fields.
+  - Execution Context: Operate under the current user privileges, enforcing field-level security and sharing rules.
+
+4.2 Handling Outcomes
+  - Success Path: Displays a success message and provides a hyperlink to the new Account record.
+  - Fault Path: Handles errors or exceptions using a fault path that guides users to a custom error screen with troubleshooting advice.
+
+5. Security and Permissions
+
+5.1 Security Compliance
+The flow respects Salesforce security configurations:
+  - Object-Level Permissions: Users require Create permissions on Account object to access the flow.
+  - Field-Level Security: Enforced by the platform to ensure sensitive data protection.
+
+5.2 Profiles and Permission Sets
+Relevant profiles and permission settings must be adjusted to grant correct access rights for initiating and completing the flow.
+
+6. Deployment Strategy
+
+6.1 Development Environment
+  - Initial Development: To be carried out in a sandbox environment.
+  - Testing: Conducted in a full or partial sandbox, validating the flow logic and security configurations.
+
+6.2 Deployment Tools
+Deployment will utilize Salesforce Change Sets or Salesforce DX:
+  - Pre-Deployment: Includes configuration validation and security review.
+  - Post-Deployment: Smoke testing in the production environment to confirm functionality.
+
+7. Testing and Validation
+
+7.1 Unit Testing
+Admin to complete thorough unit testing to ensure:
+  - All required fields are captured.
+  - Validation errors prompt correct messages and restrict progression.
+
+7.2 User Acceptance Testing (UAT)
+Sales users to perform UAT ensuring:
+  - Ease of use and functional integrity.
+  - All acceptance criteria are met.
+
+8. Assumptions and Constraints
+
+8.1 Assumptions
+  - The current Salesforce Account data model remains unchanged.
+  - Users possess the required object permissions prior to launch.
+
+8.2 Constraints
+  - No custom code interventions beyond what is necessary for exceptional error handling.
+  - Functional reliance on the standard set of Salesforce governor limits.
+
+9. Conclusion
+
+The design outlined here provides a detailed guide to the development and deployment of the Account Creation Screen Flow. By adhering to this LLD, the project will deliver a user-centric solution that simplifies Account creation, enforces data integrity, and integrates seamlessly with existing Salesforce configurations.
+
+This document is intended to serve as comprehensive guidance for developers and technical staff throughout the build and deployment phases of the project.
+
+COMPONENTS TO IMPLEMENT (extracted from LLD — generate ALL of these):
+- Flow: Account_Creation — Screen Flow that serves as the primary interface for collecting data, validating inputs, and creating new Account records
+
+IMPORTANT: Create every file listed above. Do not skip or stub any component. Each Apex class must be a separate .cls file; each LWC component must have its full .js/.html/.js-meta.xml triple.
+
+Task constraint: generate and validate implementation artifacts only. Do not run deployment or org test execution in this phase.
+
+## Project Specifications
+
+### PRD: PRD - PM creates combined PRD and feature list (draft)
+
+Product Requirements Document (PRD)  
+Project Name: Account Creation  
+Document Date: May 27, 2026  
+Author: Product Team  
+
+1. Executive Summary  
+
+This document outlines the requirements for developing a Salesforce Screen Flow that streamlines and guides users through the process of creating new Account records. The goal is to improve data accuracy, enforce required fields, reduce user errors, and provide a guided, user-friendly experience for Account creation within Salesforce.
+
+The proposed solution leverages Salesforce Screen Flow to present a structured, validated, and secure interface for users to enter Account information. Upon successful submission, a new Account record will be created in Salesforce, and the user will receive confirmation. In case of errors, users will be presented with clear and actionable error messages.
+
+2. Problem Statement  
+
+Currently, users may create Account records directly through standard Salesforce page layouts. This can lead to:
+  - Inconsistent data entry.
+  - Missing critical required fields.
+  - Increased user confusion when encountering validation errors.
+  - Limited guidance during record creation.
+
+A guided flow experience will ensure that required information is consistently captured, input validation is enforced before record creation, and users receive clear feedback throughout the process.
+
+3. Goals and Objectives  
+
+3.1 Primary Goals  
+  - Provide a structured, guided experience for creating new Account records.  
+  - Ensure all required fields (e.g., Account Name, Type, Industry) are captured.  
+  - Reduce data entry errors through proactive validation.  
+  - Improve user confidence and efficiency during Account creation.  
+
+3.2 Success Metrics  
+  - 100 percent of Accounts created via the flow include required fields.  
+  - Reduction in Account-related validation errors by at least 50 percent within 60 days of release.  
+  - Positive user feedback (average satisfaction score ≥ 4 out of 5 in post-launch survey).  
+  - Flow completion rate ≥ 95 percent.  
+
+4. Scope  
+
+4.1 In Scope  
+  - Development of a Salesforce Screen Flow for Account creation.  
+  - Input fields for required Account fields (e.g., Account Name, Type, Industry).  
+  - Validation to ensure required fields are not left blank.  
+  - Creation of a new Account record using Flow Create Records element.  
+  - Success confirmation message screen.  
+  - Error handling for record creation failure.  
+  - Deployment to production environment.  
+  - Assignment of proper permissions to relevant user profiles or permission sets.  
+
+4.2 Out of Scope  
+  - Custom Apex development (unless required for exception handling).  
+  - Integration with external systems.  
+  - Bulk Account creation via CSV or API.  
+  - Changes to Account data model beyond required configuration updates.  
+  - Advanced automation triggered post-creation (e.g., opportunity auto-creation).  
+
+5. Stakeholders  
+  - Product Manager: Owns requirements and prioritization.  
+  - CTO: Technical oversight and architecture approval.  
+  - Salesforce Administrator: Configuration and deployment.  
+  - Sales Operations: Primary business stakeholder.  
+  - End Users: Sales and service users creating Accounts.  
+  - QA Team: Testing and validation.  
+
+6. User Personas  
+
+6.1 Sales Representative  
+  - Creates new Accounts frequently.  
+  - Requires quick, guided entry.  
+  - Needs assurance that records are saved correctly.  
+
+6.2 Sales Operations Manager  
+  - Ensures data quality and completeness.  
+  - Requires consistent classification of Accounts (Type, Industry).  
+
+7. Functional Requirements  
+
+7.1 Flow Entry  
+
+FR-1: The system shall provide a Screen Flow accessible from:  
+  - A custom button or action on the Account object.  
+  - The Salesforce App Launcher or Utility Bar (to be finalized during configuration).  
+
+FR-2: Only users with appropriate object permissions (Create on Account) shall be able to launch and complete the flow.  
+
+7.2 Data Collection Screen  
+
+FR-3: The flow shall prompt the user for the following required fields:  
+  - Account Name (Text)  
+  - Type (Picklist)  
+  - Industry (Picklist)  
+
+FR-4: The flow shall dynamically use the Account object’s field definitions to ensure alignment with metadata (e.g., picklist values).  
+
+FR-5: Additional optional fields may be included (e.g., Phone, Website, Billing Address), subject to stakeholder confirmation.  
+
+7.3 Input Validation  
+
+FR-6: The flow shall enforce that Account Name is not left blank.  
+
+FR-7: Required fields (Account Name, Type, Industry) shall be configured as mandatory on the screen component level.  
+
+FR-8: If a required field is not completed, the user shall not be allowed to proceed and shall see inline validation messages.  
+
+FR-9: Field-level validation rules defined on the Account object shall also be respected.  
+
+7.4 Record Creation  
+
+FR-10: Upon successful validation, the flow shall create a new Account record using the Create Records element.  
+
+FR-11: The flow shall map each screen input field to its corresponding Account field.  
+
+FR-12: The Account record shall be created under the context of the running user and respect sharing rules and field-level security.  
+
+7.5 Confirmation and Error Handling  
+
+FR-13: Upon successful creation, the user shall see a confirmation screen with:  
+  - A success message stating that the Account was created successfully.  
+  - The Account Name displayed.  
+  - A link to the newly created Account record.  
+
+FR-14: If the record creation fails due to validation rules, missing permissions, or system errors, the flow shall:  
+  - Display a clear error message.  
+  - Avoid exposing technical stack traces.  
+  - Provide guidance to contact an administrator if the issue persists.  
+
+FR-15: The flow shall include a fault path from the Create Records element to an error screen.  
+
+8. Non-Functional Requirements  
+
+8.1 Performance  
+  - The flow should load within 2 seconds under normal system conditions.  
+  - Record creation should complete within standard Salesforce response times.  
+
+8.2 Security  
+  - The solution must respect Salesforce security model including:  
+  - Object-level permissions.  
+  - Field-Level Security (FLS).  
+  - Organization-Wide Defaults (OWD).  
+  - No elevation of privilege shall occur through the flow.  
+
+8.3 Usability  
+  - The flow shall use clear labels and field-level help text.  
+  - The layout shall be clean and logically grouped.  
+  - Error messages shall be written in plain business language.  
+
+8.4 Maintainability  
+  - The flow shall be built using best practices in Flow naming conventions and documentation.  
+  - Flow API name example: Account_Creation_Screen_Flow.  
+  - All elements shall have meaningful labels and descriptions.  
+
+9. Data Model Considerations  
+  - The solution will leverage the standard Account object.  
+  - Required fields shall align with the Account object configuration.  
+  - Any new required field introduced at the object level must be reflected in the flow.  
+  - Field-Level Security must be reviewed to ensure users can access required fields.  
+
+10. Technical Design Overview  
+
+10.1 Architecture  
+  - Salesforce Screen Flow (Auto-Layout recommended for maintainability).  
+  - Create Records element for Account object.  
+  - Fault connectors for error handling.  
+
+10.2 Deployment  
+  - Developed in a sandbox environment.  
+  - Tested in a full or partial sandbox.  
+  - Deployed via Change Sets or Salesforce DX (to be defined).  
+
+10.3 Governor Limits  
+  - Minimal DML operations (single record creation).  
+  - No loops or unnecessary queries.  
+  - Designed to operate within standard Flow limits.  
+
+11. Assumptions  
+  - Users have Create permission on Account.  
+  - Required fields are limited to Account Name, Type, and Industry unless otherwise specified.  
+  - No external integrations are required at launch.  
+  - Standard Salesforce Account object is in use (no custom object replacement).  
+
+12. Risks and Mitigation  
+
+Risk: Additional required fields may be added later.  
+Mitigation: Build flow to easily accommodate additional required inputs.  
+
+Risk: Conflicts with existing validation rules.  
+Mitigation: Conduct validation rule review prior to deployment.  
+
+Risk: User resistance to change.  
+Mitigation: Provide training and in-app guidance.  
+
+13. Release Plan  
+
+Phase 1: Requirements Finalization  
+  - Stakeholder validation of required and optional fields.  
+
+Phase 2: Build  
+  - Flow configuration in sandbox.  
+  - Security and permission review.  
+
+Phase 3: Testing  
+  - Unit testing by admin.  
+  - UAT with Sales users.  
+  - Validation of error handling scenarios.  
+
+Phase 4: Deployment  
+  - Production deployment via approved deployment method.  
+  - Post-deployment smoke testing.  
+
+Phase 5: Post-Release Monitoring  
+  - Monitor flow error rates.  
+  - Collect user feedback.  
+
+14. Acceptance Criteria  
+
+AC-1: The flow prompts for Account Name, Type, and Industry.  
+AC-2: The flow prevents submission when Account Name is blank.  
+AC-3: Upon successful completion, a new Account record is created in Salesforce.  
+AC-4: The user sees a confirmation message upon successful creation.  
+AC-5: If record creation fails, the user sees a clear and user-friendly error message.  
+
+15. Open Questions  
+  - Should additional fields be mandatory at launch?  
+  - Should the flow replace the standard “New Account” button entirely?  
+  - Should post-creation automation (e.g., task creation) be included in a future phase?  
+
+16. Conclusion  
+
+The Account Creation Screen Flow will provide a structured, validated, and user-friendly mechanism for creating new Account records in Salesforce. By guiding users through required fields, enforcing validation rules, and providing clear success and error messaging, the solution will improve data quality, reduce user errors, and enhance overall system usability.  
+
+This PRD serves as the foundation for design, build, testing, and deployment activities for the Account Creation project.
+
+---
+
+### LLD: LLD - Sr Developer creates combined technical design (draft)
